<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
    <PropertyGroup>
        <OutputType>Library</OutputType>
        <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
        <RootNamespace>Bennewitz.Ninja.LayeredEditors.Avalonia</RootNamespace>
        <AssemblyName>LayeredEditors.Avalonia</AssemblyName>
        <AvaloniaUseCompiledBindingsByDefault>true</AvaloniaUseCompiledBindingsByDefault>
        <Description>
            Avalonia view-models and views for layered-scope settings editors.
            Depends only on LayeredEditors.Abstractions + Avalonia + CommunityToolkit.Mvvm —
            no JSON, no domain coupling. Consumers supply IEditorWorkspace / IEditorSchema
            implementations that adapt their own data source.
        </Description>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!-- The solution's friend grants, in one shared file next to the .slnx. LINKED, not
         copied, by a relative path with forward slashes so Linux and macOS resolve it the
         same way Windows does. Read that file before adding a name: it is compiled into
         every assembly, so a grant there applies to all of them. -->
    <ItemGroup>
        <Compile Include="../../AssemblyInfo.InternalsVisibleTo.cs" Link="Properties/AssemblyInfo.InternalsVisibleTo.cs"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <!--
        JetBrains Mono NL, bundled rather than named.

        ⛔ WHY BUNDLED: a font STACK is a request, not a guarantee. The same markup rendered
        three different faces across the three platforms, and on Windows the severity glyphs
        resolved to TWO faces at once — U+26A0 to Segoe UI Emoji (a COLOUR BITMAP, which also
        ignores Foreground outright) and U+2297 to Segoe UI Symbol. Shipping the file is the
        only way the drawn result is the same everywhere.

        ⭐ NL = the no-ligature cut, and that is a correctness choice, not a taste one. This
        app's monospace surfaces show the literal bytes of a config file, a path or a command;
        a ligature would compose `!=` or `->` into one glyph, so what the user reads would stop
        matching what the file contains. Prose keeps the proportional UI font and its ordinary
        typographic ligatures.

        ⭐ BOTH CUTS SHIP, and they are not interchangeable:

          • JetBrains Mono NL  — no ligatures. Every surface that shows the literal contents
            of a file: config values, JSON, paths, commands, permission rules, markdown code
            blocks. This is the default, and AppMonoFontFamily points at it.
          • JetBrains Mono     — ligatures. For surfaces read for COMPREHENSION rather than
            byte-fidelity. Reserved for the log viewer's planned readable/byte-exact toggle;
            no surface binds it yet, which is deliberate rather than an oversight.

        ⚠ ASYMMETRIC WEIGHTS, on purpose. NL ships Regular, SemiBold and Bold because markup
        asks for exactly those three. The ligature cut ships Regular and Bold only, because
        nothing asks for its SemiBold — a weight nothing requests costs every RID archive
        ~270 KB to sit unused. Add one when a surface actually needs it.

        ⚠ COST, stated rather than discovered: five files, ~1.2 MB uncompressed, embedded in a
        library that is also a published NuGet package.

        ⭐ TRIM-SAFE by construction: AvaloniaResource embeds these as resources, which ILLink
        does not strip, and nothing here is reached by reflection — the URI is resolved by
        Avalonia's asset loader against an assembly that is plainly referenced. Avalonia.Fonts.Inter
        already rides the same mechanism through this repo's trimmed six-RID publish.

        ⚠ OFL 1.1 requires the licence to travel with the font, so OFL.txt is embedded beside
        it rather than left in the source tree only — the app publishes as a SINGLE FILE, so a
        loose sibling file would not reach the user at all.
    -->
    <ItemGroup>
        <!-- Byte-exact cut: the default for every literal-value surface. -->
        <AvaloniaResource Include="Assets/Fonts/JetBrainsMonoNL-Regular.ttf" />
        <AvaloniaResource Include="Assets/Fonts/JetBrainsMonoNL-SemiBold.ttf" />
        <AvaloniaResource Include="Assets/Fonts/JetBrainsMonoNL-Bold.ttf" />
        <!-- Ligature cut: bound by nothing yet, held for the log viewer's reading mode. -->
        <AvaloniaResource Include="Assets/Fonts/JetBrainsMono-Regular.ttf" />
        <AvaloniaResource Include="Assets/Fonts/JetBrainsMono-Bold.ttf" />
        <AvaloniaResource Include="Assets/Fonts/OFL.txt" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <PackageReference Include="Avalonia" Version="12.1.3" />
        <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Controls.DataGrid" Version="12.1.2" />
        <PackageReference Include="CommunityToolkit.Mvvm" Version="8.4.2" />
        <!--
          Opt-in Semi.Avalonia theme bundle. Consumers that want Semi styling include
          avares://LayeredEditors.Avalonia/Themes/SemiBundle.axaml from their App.axaml;
          consumers that prefer Fluent (or another theme) simply omit that include.
          PrivateAssets="" lets the Semi assemblies flow transitively to consuming apps.
        -->
        <PackageReference Include="Semi.Avalonia" Version="12.1.0" PrivateAssets="" />
        <PackageReference Include="Semi.Avalonia.DataGrid" Version="12.1.0" PrivateAssets=""/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="..\LayeredEditors.Abstractions\LayeredEditors.Abstractions.csproj"/>
        <ProjectReference Include="..\LayeredEditors.ViewModels\LayeredEditors.ViewModels.csproj"/>
    </ItemGroup>
</Project>
